  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/02d46e13c84fc575f489a9870f3b1213eb3d180f3f5ef6e6a546d3acfe4b2282.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound  as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=2\\cdot \\infty =\\infty .\n$$",
